    "83070d7b-763c-4195-9f88-29e973ce22fb": {
      "id": "83070d7b-763c-4195-9f88-29e973ce22fb",
      "message": {
        "id": "83070d7b-763c-4195-9f88-29e973ce22fb",
        "author": {
          "role": "assistant",
          "name": null,
          "metadata": {}
        },
        "create_time": 1776773467.882951,
        "update_time": null,
        "content": {
          "content_type": "text",
          "parts": [
            "“Space” usually refers to the vast expanse beyond Earth’s atmosphere—the universe that contains everything: stars, planets, galaxies, and the invisible structures that hold them together.\n\nAt a basic level, space is not truly empty. Even though it’s often called a vacuum, it contains tiny amounts of particles, radiation, and fields. It’s where objects like entity[\"astronomical_object\",\"Earth\",\"planet\"], entity[\"astronomical_object\",\"Mars\",\"planet\"], and our entity[\"astronomical_object\",\"Milky Way\",\"galaxy\"] exist.\n\nA few key ways to understand space:\n\n- **No air**: There’s no atmosphere like on Earth, so you can’t breathe and sound doesn’t travel normally.  \n- **Microgravity**: Objects float because there’s very little gravitational pull compared to Earth.  \n- **Vast distances**: Space is unimaginably large—light from distant stars can take millions or billions of years to reach us.  \n- **Darkness and radiation**: Without atmosphere, space is mostly dark and filled with cosmic radiation.\n\nScientifically, space is also tied to the idea of **spacetime** (from entity[\"scientific_concept\",\"Theory of Relativity\",\"physics\"]), meaning space and time are interconnected and can bend around massive objects like stars and black holes.\n\nIf you want, I can break it down in a simpler way, or go deeper into things like black holes, galaxies, or how space began."
          ]
        },
