        "update_time": null,
        "content": {
          "content_type": "text",
          "parts": [
            "Yes—there’s a lot in space that we **can’t see directly**.\n\nOur eyes only detect a small part of light (called visible light), but the universe contains things that don’t emit light, or emit it in ways we can’t see.\n\nHere are some key examples:\n\n**1. Dark matter**  \nScientists believe most of the matter in the universe is **entity[\"scientific_concept\",\"Dark matter\",\"invisible matter in the universe\"]**.  \n- It doesn’t give off light, so we can’t see it  \n- We know it exists because of its **gravitational effects** on galaxies  \n\n**2. Dark energy**  \nEven more mysterious is **entity[\"scientific_concept\",\"Dark energy\",\"unknown force causing expansion of universe\"]**.  \n- It’s thought to be pushing the universe to expand faster  \n- We can’t see or directly measure it—only its effects  \n\n**3. Black holes**  \nA **entity[\"scientific_concept\",\"Black hole\",\"region where gravity prevents light escape\"]** doesn’t emit light.  \n- You can’t see it directly  \n- But you can detect it by how it affects nearby stars or gas  \n\n**4. Invisible radiation**  \nSpace is full of energy we can’t see, like:  \n- Infrared (heat)  \n- X-rays  \n- Radio waves  \n\nScientists use special telescopes to detect these.\n\n---\n\nSo yes—**a huge part of space is invisible to us**.  \nIn fact, what we *can* see (stars, planets, galaxies) is only a small fraction of what’s really out there.\n\nIf you want, we can go deeper into whether this could include life or other unknown forms of existence—that’s where things get really interesting."
          ]
        },
